---
import { adsEnv } from '@lib/ads-env';

/**
 * Journey by Mediavine's script wrapper - the whole of the Journey integration,
 * beside the AdSense publisher tag in GoogleConsent.astro while the site
 * applies to both partners.
 *
 * One tag in the head of every page is all Journey asks of a custom-built site:
 * its wrapper finds the article body and the sidebar by the `journey-content`
 * and `journey-sidebar` landmarks (ArticleBody.astro, blog/[slug].astro), places
 * and refreshes its units itself, and shows its own consent notice where the
 * law requires one. Nothing here is consent-gated by the site, because the
 * partner owns that decision end to end; the site's Privacy preferences dialog
 * decides analytics only.
 *
 * It sits in the head, as Mediavine asks, and ahead of the analytics bundle:
 * the wrapper is what loads Journey's consent platform, so it has to be on the
 * page before Google Analytics reads a Consent Mode signal from it.
 *
 * The attributes are the ones Mediavine issues and must not be optimised away:
 * `data-noptimize` and `data-cfasync` tell minifiers and Cloudflare's Rocket
 * Loader to leave the tag alone, and their crawler checks for the tag as issued.
 *
 * Renders nothing on a build without a site id - which today is every build,
 * because Journey issues the id only once it approves the site - and on every
 * preview and local `pnpm dev` (see `@lib/ads-env`). Journey requires
 * exclusivity once it serves ads, so the day it launches the AdSense tag goes.
 */
const { mediavineScriptSrc } = adsEnv();
---
{mediavineScriptSrc && (
  <script is:inline type="text/javascript" async="async" data-noptimize="1" data-cfasync="false" src={mediavineScriptSrc}></script>
)}
